          dealing: 'The dealer deals 5 cards face down to each player (4 or 3 cards in some variants to accommodate more players).',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by having a card in your hand that is the same suit as and higher in rank than a card dealt from the remaining deck.',
          gameplay: "1. Starting to the left of the dealer, each player makes a bet against the current pot.\n2. **Betting**: You must bet at least the ante and may bet up to the total amount in the pot.\n3. **The Reveal**: Once the bet is placed, the dealer 'burns' one card and then reveals the next card from the deck.\n4. **Outcome**: - If you have a card of the SAME suit and HIGHER rank than the revealed card, you WIN and take your stake + an equal amount from the pot. - If you do NOT have a card of that suit or your cards of that suit are LOWER, you LOSE and your stake is added to the pot.",
          keyRules: [
            'Follow the legal action constraints defined for High Card Pool.',
            'Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Players bet against the central pot.',
            'Winning condition: Win the pot by having a card in your hand that is the same suit as and higher in rank than a card dealt from the remaining deck.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Only bet the maximum when you have high cards in multiple suits. If you have an Ace in 3 suits, you have a very high probability of winning.',
          intermediate: "Calculate the 'outs'. If you have only one suit (e.g., Spades) but it's the Ace, your chance of winning is roughly 1 in 4 (if a Spade is dealt, you win; if not, you lose). Betting the pot on a 25% chance is mathematically unsound.",
          advanced: "Watch the burn cards and previous players' turns. Card counting can slightly improve your odds by knowing which high cards have already been removed from the deck.",
          tips: [
            'Conservative betting on low-probability hands keeps you in the game longer.',
            'The house edge is zero if you are the dealer in a fair social rotation, but the variance is very high.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
